      "taxonomies": [
        {
          "name": "criteria",
          "version": "1",
          "informationUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req",
          "organization": "Fluidattacks",
          "taxa": [
            {
              "id": "186",
              "name": "Use the principle of least privilege",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The principle of least privilege must be applied when creating new objects and roles, setting access permissions, and accessing other systems.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Systems should have a set of roles with different levels of privilege to access resources. Users and applications should always have a role with the minimum level of privilege required to execute their functions. A violation of this may become a new vulnerability or leverage for causing a greater impact when exploiting other vulnerabilities.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/186"
            },
            {
              "id": "265",
              "name": "Restrict access to critical processes",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must restrict access to system functions that execute critical business processes, allowing only authorized users.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Systems must enforce access controls on trusted enforcement points. They must also have a clear definition of user privileges and roles. Functions that execute critical business processes should only be available for authenticated users with roles that have the required privileges.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/265"
            },
            {
              "id": "177",
              "name": "Avoid caching and temporary files",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must not store sensitive information in temporary files or cache memory.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Applications sometimes reside in or get consumed by environments in which caching is possible. Caching helps performance or makes certain actions more comfortable for the application users. However, cached information is often more susceptible to being exposed or corrupted. Thus, avoiding cache memory and temporary files helps protect sensitive information.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/177"
            },
            {
              "id": "173",
              "name": "Discard unsafe inputs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must discard all potentially harmful information received via data inputs.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Technological devices and, in particular, applications must be able to notice if the received information does not correspond to its operational purposes, in order to treat it properly (for example, rejecting and/or generating timely alerts) and ensure that it does not impact the operation negatively. Typical examples of this are: SQL queries, JavaScript code, OS commands or LDAP queries in fields and application parameters; text with undesired special characters and their possible combinations in fields and parameters; files manipulated in structure and extension to be loaded in an application or technology artifact; and in general any type of information that does not correspond to the requested format. A large amount of the incoming traffic (which does not correspond to operational purposes) of a technological artifact must also be considered, and controls and proper treatment must be applied.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/173"
            },
            {
              "id": "134",
              "name": "Store passwords with salt",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must store passwords with different key derivations (salt).\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The use of salts introduces an extra layer of complexity for attackers attempting to crack password hashes.\nThis requirement is a fundamental practice for protecting user credentials, mitigating common attack vectors, such as rainbow tables, dictionary or brute force attacks.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/134"
            },
            {
              "id": "135",
              "name": "Passwords with random salt",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Salt values in passwords must be random and have a minimum length of 48 bits.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "By being random and having a minimum length of 48 bits the salt complexity is strong enough to mitigate the risk of a successful attack over the user's password.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/135"
            },
            {
              "id": "185",
              "name": "Encrypt sensitive information",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "All stored sensitive information must be encrypted.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Systems usually stores personal data, i.e., Personally Identifiable Information (PII), medical records, credentials and other types of sensitive information. All of these must be encrypted before being stored using safe cryptographic mechanisms. This is also applicable when personal information must be temporarily stored in the client-side storage. The encryption prevents unauthorized actors that may have accessed the storage system from obtaining the information.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/185"
            },
            {
              "id": "229",
              "name": "Request access credentials",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must request at least one username and password from every actor that tries to authenticate.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Sometimes systems have information and other resources that are not considered public. These resources should be protected by a secure authentication mechanism that prevents unauthorized actors from accessing them. The authentication mechanism should request at least a username and a password.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/229"
            },
            {
              "id": "264",
              "name": "Request authentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must require authentication for all resources, except for the consultation or visualization of those specifically classified as public.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Sometimes systems have information and other resources that are not considered public. These resources should be protected by a secure authentication mechanism that prevents unauthorized actors from accessing them.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/264"
            },
            {
              "id": "300",
              "name": "Mask sensitive data",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Business sensitive data (passwords, credit card numbers, CVV, etc.) must be masked.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Applications usually handle personal information, such as credit card numbers, CVV, personal identifications, social security numbers, etc. The exposure of this data could severely affect their owners and so it must be considered sensitive and be specifically protected. Masking is a mechanism that contributes to this protection.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/300"
            },
            {
              "id": "305",
              "name": "Prioritize token usage",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "All systems related to credit cards must use tokens instead of storing card information.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When processing transactions, systems can use tokens instead of the actual credit card information. This allows secure communication between systems without exposing sensitive details during transactions.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/305"
            },
            {
              "id": "255",
              "name": "Allow access only to the necessary ports",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Network segments and servers with applications or content must allow access only to the necessary ports.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Unnecessary open ports increase the likelihood of exposure to exploits and attacks targeting specific services or applications. Closing unused ports mitigates the risk of exploitation and limits the potential impact of security vulnerabilities.\nAdditionally, proper segmentation of network resources and restriction of ports contribute are important parts of a secure network architecture.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/255"
            },
            {
              "id": "181",
              "name": "Transmit data using secure protocols",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The transmission of sensitive information and the execution of sensitive functions must be performed through secure protocols.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A system can send information through a non-encrypted channel using insecure protocols. The use of these protocols makes it easier to perform a man-in-the-middle attack (MitM) to intercept and modify the information. Examples of such insecure protocols are HTTP, FTP, POP3 and Telnet.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/181"
            },
            {
              "id": "148",
              "name": "Set minimum size of asymmetric encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The asymmetric encryption mechanism must use a minimum key size of 2048 bits.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The key size in asymmetric encryption is directly correlated with the strength of the encryption. Larger key sizes provide a higher level of security because they increase the complexity of the mathematical problem that an attack is intended to solve and break the encryption.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/148"
            },
            {
              "id": "149",
              "name": "Set minimum size of symmetric encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The symmetric encryption mechanism must use a minimum key size of 128 bits.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "As the key size increases, the number of possible key combinations grows significantly. Each additional bit in the key size doubles the number of possible keys. A 128-bit key provides a larger key space compared to shorter key sizes, making it more resistant to brute force attacks.\nWidely used symmetric encryption algorithms, such as Advanced Encryption Standard (AES), use a 128-bit key size as the default or minimum recommended key length.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/149"
            },
            {
              "id": "150",
              "name": "Set minimum size for hash functions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use hash functions with a minimum size of 256 bits.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Systems must use hash functions with a minimum size of 256 bits because it is important for achieving a high level of security and resistance against various cryptographic attacks.\nIt provides a robust foundation for cryptographic protocols and password hashing implementations.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/150"
            },
            {
              "id": "336",
              "name": "Disable insecure TLS versions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must disable out-of-date or insecure versions of SSL and TLS protocols, algorithms and ciphers.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "All communications between the client and the server should take place over channels that are protected and encrypted. In order to guarantee this, out-of-date or insecure protocols such as SSLv2, SSLv3, or TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 should be disabled. The latest version of TLS should be used and communications should not be allowed to fall back on insecure or unencrypted protocols.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/336"
            },
            {
              "id": "075",
              "name": "Record exceptional events in logs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must register all exceptional and security events in logs.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The organization must properly record the exceptional and security events in duly protected logs (confidentiality), considering that an event of this type should not show confidential or detailed information of the problem in error messages to prevent the use of that information by an attacker or malicious user.\nRecorded events allow error pages to display simple generic messages, alerting end users that an error has occurred, with some option to contact support.\nThe details of how to address these problems should be kept securely stored in a previously defined log storage. When an event of this type is not properly recorded, a malicious behavior can be proven difficult to detect or a forensic analysis can be obstructed in case an attack is successful.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/075"
            },
            {
              "id": "376",
              "name": "Register severity level",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must register the severity level for each exceptional and security event.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The system must implement a rigorous log methodology. Event logging is a fundamental practice in information security and system monitoring. It enables organizations to prioritize actions, respond adequately to critical incidents, and maintain a comprehensive record of security events for analysis, reporting, and even compliance purposes.\nSeverity levels are used to categorize the importance or impact of an event. They typically go, for example, from low to high, with each level indicating the urgency or criticality of the event.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/376"
            },
            {
              "id": "377",
              "name": "Store logs based on valid regulation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The organization must store logs, at least, from the ocurrency of the event on the system to the time stipulated by valid regulation for that specific system.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The organization must take into account the regulations that apply to its systems. Different regulations and standards may have specific requirements for log retention periods.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/377"
            },
            {
              "id": "378",
              "name": "Use of log management system",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The log management must be made by the operating system or by an external system separated from the application.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Log management brings several benefits to an organization. The records of events or transactions can be valuable for troubleshooting, monitoring and security analysis.\nThe requirement emphasizes the separation of log management from the application. This separation helps to ensure that log data is not compromised, tampered, or affected by issues within the application itself.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/378"
            },
            {
              "id": "323",
              "name": "Exclude unverifiable files",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Binary and other types of files, which are often not audited for security purposes, should not be stored in the source code repository.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Binary files usually have a file size greater than their source counterpart, which can eventually affect a repository performance. Changes done to them are often hard to track for versioning tools or make no sense for a reviewer. Furthermore, security audits on binary files are more complicated or simply not performed, and these could contain serious vulnerabilities such as backdoors, rootkits and exposed sensitive information.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/323"
            },
            {
              "id": "266",
              "name": "Disable insecure functionalities",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The organization must disable or carefully control the insecure functions of a system (system hardening).\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Sometimes, platforms include functionalities that are not required or could be harmful for some applications built on top of or residing in them. Other times, applications are developed including functionalities that allow actions that could be considered insecure. All these functionalities should be disabled or otherwise controlled to prevent them from compromising the system's security. Furthermore, the system must enforce those controls on trusted enforcement points such as access control gateways, severs and serverless functions.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/266"
            },
            {
              "id": "329",
              "name": "Keep client-side storage without sensitive data",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Personal, sensitive and session data must not be stored in the client-side storage (localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies without security attributes, mobile device unencrypted storage, etc.).\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Data placed in the localStorage persists after a session is closed and thus, any actor with access to the browser will be able to obtain it. Furthermore, data in the localStorage or in the sessionStorage is visible to scripts that are running on the browser, and these scripts could belong to malicious third-parties. Therefore, no sensitive or session information should be stored in the client-side storage.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/329"
            },
            {
              "id": "375",
              "name": "Remove sensitive data from client-side applications",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Access codes, tokens or credentials should be removed from client-side applications. If its needed, the associated service should support Access Control Lists based on the expected origin.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Storing sensitive information on the client side increases security risks, as it could be exposed, intercepted, or tampered by malicious actors.\nAlso, the Access Control Lists (ACLs) is a useful mechanism used to control access to resources based on specified rules mitigating the risk of unauthorized access to sensitive data.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/375"
            },
            {
              "id": "145",
              "name": "Protect system cryptographic keys",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The systems private asymmetric or symmetric keys must be protected and should not be exposed.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The systems cryptographic keys are essential for maintaining the confidentiality and integrity of transactions and communications. Their exposure may cause business information leakages, loss of data integrity and loss of trust due to the inability to differentiate server traffic from malicious traffic. Therefore, these keys must be protected and managed following industry-verified standards.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/145"
            },
            {
              "id": "156",
              "name": "Source code without sensitive information",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The source code must not contain sensitive information.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive data is often included in the source code during early development stages for practicality or due to a lack of early architecture. This data includes credentials, secrets, cryptographic keys, personal identification numbers and other personal information. Following secure programming practices, none of this information should be present in the source code, as a leak could put critical systems in jeopardy.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/156"
            },
            {
              "id": "172",
              "name": "Encrypt connection strings",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system should load encrypted database connection strings from a protected configuration file that resides separately from the source code.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Database connection strings are very sensitive information because they contain credentials that often have high privileges over the systems database. Thus, these strings should not be part of the systems source code and should not be stored in plain text. They should be encrypted using a secure cryptographic algorithm and the encryption key should also be protected.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/172"
            },
            {
              "id": "077",
              "name": "Avoid disclosing technical information",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The application must not disclose internal system information such as stack traces, SQL sentence fragments, database names or table names.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Applications should fail safely whenever an unexpected event occurs. Error message presentation is part of this safe management. Therefore, specific technical information should not be presented to unauthorized users, as this could be leveraged by attackers to further exploit other vulnerabilities.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/077"
            },
            {
              "id": "176",
              "name": "Restrict system objects",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must restrict access to system objects that have sensitive content. It should only allow access to authorized users.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Applications usually handle personal and confidential information, such as personal identifications, social security numbers, credentials and health histories. This data should be protected as a fundamental right, and therefore be stored and transmitted using secure mechanisms that prevent access to it by unauthorized actors. Furthermore, the access control model and role assignment policy must be implemented taking these restrictions into consideration.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/176"
            },
            {
              "id": "365",
              "name": "Avoid exposing technical information",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Technical information (product name, version, configuration) of exposed services should not be accessible.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Attackers usually perform reconnaissance to gather information about the target system. Exposing detailed technical information makes it easier for attackers to identify potential vulnerabilities or outdated software versions that may have known security issues.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/365"
            },
            {
              "id": "095",
              "name": "Define users with privileges",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The users that will access the system with administrator or root privileges must be defined.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Systems should have a set of roles with different levels of privilege to access resources. The privileges of each role must be clearly defined and the role of each user should also be clearly stated. That includes the set of users that will have administrator or root privileges, as this should not be a default role.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/095"
            },
            {
              "id": "096",
              "name": "Set user's required privileges",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The privileges required by the users who will access the system must be defined.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Systems should have a set of roles with different levels of privileges to access resources. The privileges of each role must be clearly defined and the role of each user should also be clearly stated.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/096"
            },
            {
              "id": "039",
              "name": "Define maximum file size",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The files manipulated by the system and the users must have a defined maximum file size (5MB recommended).\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a system allows users to upload or attach files for storage, the maximum file size limit must be defined for these files, in order to avoid issues involving the availability of the service and to reduce the chance of an attacker uploading a file containing malicious software.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/039"
            },
            {
              "id": "223",
              "name": "Uniform distribution in random numbers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system's random numbers must be generated using a uniform distribution.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The system's cryptographic keys are essential for maintaining the confidentiality and integrity of transactions and communications. Some of these keys and other critical elements are generated using random numbers. In these cases, the random numbers themselves must be generated using secure mechanisms that guarantee a random distribution.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/223"
            },
            {
              "id": "224",
              "name": "Use secure cryptographic mechanisms",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must use the most secure cryptographic mechanism provided by the platform (e.g., java.security.SecureRandom) for random number generation used in critical processes (e.g., ID generation, code mapping, cryptographic keys).\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The system's cryptographic keys are essential for maintaining the confidentiality and integrity of transactions and communications. Some of these keys and other critical elements are generated using random numbers. In these cases, the random numbers themselves must be generated using secure mechanisms, which have often already been implemented by the platform.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/224"
            },
            {
              "id": "346",
              "name": "Use initialization vectors once",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system should use initialization vectors, nonces and other single use numbers only once with a given encryption key.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The system's cryptographic keys are essential for maintaining the confidentiality and integrity of transactions and communications. Some encryption mechanisms use initialization vectors to reduce the chances that a message will be decrypted. These vectors should be generated using cryptographically secure random number generators and should only be used once with a given encryption key.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/346"
            },
            {
              "id": "062",
              "name": "Define standard configurations",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The organization must define standard configurations that correct all known vulnerabilities. These configurations must also be consistent with industry standards.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "System configuration is essential when it comes to security issues. The system must follow the industry's standard configurations that prevent all known vulnerabilities. These settings also contribute to ensuring the ongoing confidentiality, integrity, availability and resilience of systems and services.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/062"
            },
            {
              "id": "349",
              "name": "Include HTTP security headers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must attach properly-configured HTTP security headers to its requests and responses.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP security headers can be used to increase the overall security of an application. They are very effective at preventing the exploitation of several common vulnerabilities. For this reason, they should be configured as strictly as possible and included in all server requests and responses.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/349"
            },
            {
              "id": "169",
              "name": "Use parameterized queries",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The system must use parameterized queries or stored procedures to create dynamic sentences (e.g., java.sql.PreparedStatement).\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "One of the main reasons for using parameterized queries or stored procedures is to prevent SQL injection attacks. When applications concatenate user input directly into SQL statements, attackers may inject malicious SQL code, potentially leading to unauthorized access or data manipulation. Parameterized queries ensure that user input is treated as data, not as executable code, mitigating the risk of SQL injection.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/169"
            },
            {
              "id": "127",
              "name": "Store hashed passwords",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Passwords must be hashed before being stored using secure hash algorithms such as `PBKDF2` and `bcrypt`.\n"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A hash function maps data of arbitrary size to fixed-size values. It conceals sensitive information as it is often not possible to reverse hashed texts. Hashing passwords helps to prevent unauthorized actors from obtaining them when accessing the storage system.\n"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://db.fluidattacks.com/req/127"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}